<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened runtime, which notarisation requires and which
  the player does not otherwise want.

  One entry, and it is not optional: mpv's scripting runtime is LuaJIT 2.1, and
  `lua/zoompan.lua` is what makes zoom and pan land in a single frame (there is
  no way to set several mpv properties atomically from outside — see gotcha 4 in
  CLAUDE.md). The hardened runtime kills a process that executes a page it did
  not sign, and a JIT writes exactly such pages.

  Measured rather than reasoned about, because the obvious key is the wrong one.
  A probe that loads the bundled libmpv with zoompan.lua, signed four ways and
  run under the hardened runtime:

    (nothing)                          SIGKILL — EXC_BAD_ACCESS, "Invalid Page",
                                       CODESIGNING, faulting address inside a
                                       private VM_ALLOCATE region mapped r-x
    allow-jit                          SIGKILL — identical
    allow-unsigned-executable-memory   runs; the script loads
    both                               runs

  So `com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit` — the entitlement whose name matches the
  problem — does not fix it, and is not here. It authorises `MAP_JIT`, and this
  LuaJIT does not use it: it maps executable memory the ordinary way, which is
  what the blunter key covers. Adding both would lift one restriction for
  nothing.

  Deliberately nothing else. Every entitlement is a restriction lifted, and the
  hardened runtime does not restrict what this application actually does —
  networking, file access and spawning the bundled ffmpeg are all unaffected by
  it (those belong to the App Sandbox, which is not in use here).

  One that is NOT here, and the reason is the same as why `macos-build.sh`
  turns the hardened runtime on only for a signed build:

    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
        Not needed, because the ~35 dylibs in Resources are signed with the same
        Developer ID as the binary that loads them — which is exactly what
        library validation asks for.

        It is needed for an *ad-hoc* build, and cannot help it: ad-hoc code has
        no Team ID, so there is nothing for the check to match and dyld refuses
        the first library at launch ("mapping process and mapped file
        (non-platform) have different Team IDs"). That is why the ad-hoc
        fallback drops the hardened runtime instead of lifting this restriction
        for every user of a signed build.

        Note the probe above does not see any of this — it `dlopen`s the
        libraries, and the app links them. A library set that loads in isolation
        says nothing about the bundle; only launching the bundle does.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
